text:
anna: heads-up — onboarding sample app on Pixel 8 / Android 14 is intermittently freezing the UI during image uploads. can't repro locally on emulator, happens on device.

mike (sdk): freeze as in ANR or just spinner stuck?

anna: spinner - not ANR. main thread blocked for ~200-500ms when a network retry kicks in after 401. users notice jank during upload progress.

sophie (android): do you call retrofit synchronously on main thread anywhere?

anna: no, using enqueue. logs show a flurry: `401`, then our `Authenticator` runs, does a blocking refresh (HTTP call), then next request throws `java.net.SocketException: Connection reset`. UI stutter correlates with the refresh call duration.

julian (eng-runtime): hmm Authenticator executes on an OkHttp network thread, it shouldn't block the main thread directly. Could the progress callback be posted from that same thread and doing heavy work on the main looper?

anna: progress updates are posted to main via handler. nothing heavy. i instrumented and the OS reports main thread busy while the refresh is happening.

mike (sdk): two suspects: 1) our Interceptor/Authenticator is accidentally doing work on the main thread (e.g., calling some synchronized method that hits UI code) 2) OkHttp dispatcher saturates and the progress postback uses Handler.sendMessageAtFrontOfQueue which can starve.

sophie (android): can you paste the Authenticator code?

anna: sure, simplified:

```
class TokenAuthenticator implements Authenticator {
  @Override
  public Request authenticate(Route route, Response response) throws IOException {
    synchronized (TokenStore.class) {
      String token = TokenStore.get();
      if (token != null && !isExpired(token)) return response.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer " + token).build();
      // blocking refresh
      Token newToken = AuthApi.syncRefresh(); // Retrofit call .execute()
      TokenStore.set(newToken.value);
      return response.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer " + newToken.value).build();
    }
  }
}
```

mike (sdk): there's the problem: `synchronized` on TokenStore can block any thread that also synchronizes for reads/writes — are you using that same lock path from the main thread for reads or progress callbacks?

anna: we only read TokenStore in an Interceptor which is called on the network thread. main thread shouldn't touch it.

julian (eng-runtime): another angle: `AuthApi.syncRefresh()` is a blocking Retrofit call that will acquire a connection from OkHttp's connection pool. If the pool is exhausted (because other in-flight requests are streaming or keeping connections), that blocking call can cause other callbacks to stall and indirectly affect main thread work (Looper messages queued from network callbacks). Also, non-repeatable streaming bodies cause Dispatcher to queue and stall.

sophie (android): do you use chunked streaming for uploads (e.g., `RequestBody` that reads from file stream without buffering)?

anna: yes, large images are streaming via `RequestBody` that reads from file input stream. we avoid buffering to save memory.

mike (sdk): streaming bodies are non-repeatable. OkHttp's retry logic may need to re-send, which isn't possible so it fails and falls back, causing a weird sequence. Combine that with blocking refresh calls and you can get weird ordering and thread handoffs.

anna: any quick mitigation until we fix architecture?

julian (eng-runtime): short term: 1) make the refresh call use a separate OkHttpClient instance with its own connection pool so it won't compete for sockets with streaming uploads. 2) avoid heavy synchronized blocks that can cascade. 3) mark progress posting as `post()` instead of `postAtFrontOfQueue`.

mike (sdk): code snippet for separate client approach:

```
// small client for auth only
OkHttpClient authClient = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .connectionPool(new ConnectionPool(2, 5, TimeUnit.MINUTES))
  .dispatcher(new Dispatcher(Executors.newCachedThreadPool()))
  .build();

// use Retrofit built with authClient inside TokenAuthenticator
```

sophie (android): also consider converting the Authenticator to return null and surface a 401 to app logic once, then let your app trigger a single refresh flow (coalesced) and retry the call using `call.clone()` after token is refreshed. This gives you more control over threading and avoids blocking stack inside OkHttp internals.

anna: good idea. coalescing refreshes is already in our plan but we were trying to be clever in Authenticator. looks like that backfired for streaming payloads.

mike (sdk): related SDK note — add a sample snippet to the onboarding app showing `authClient` pattern and a safe Authenticator -> app-refresh fallback. we should also add a test that simulates streaming + auth refresh concurrency.

anna: i'll prototype the auth-client quickfix and try the `return null` flow. i'll paste repro logs after. thanks! :party_parrot: 

ci-bot: deploy check — sample-app build passed on CI (branch: fix/auth-client-214999)

julian (eng-runtime): great. if you post logs I can sketch a unit test that reproduces the connection handoff pattern.

anna: will do, thanks everyone.
